		 <td>                            
	                                   

<P>&#9;Kheldan was not only Khondoran’s aprentice, he was his ward. Twenty-five years ago, Kheldan’s family had been attacked by the monster. Kheldan’s father, shaman of great renown, battled the creature, while his mother, placed the baby’s pram on their dog-sled and whipped the dogs away.</P><br><P>&#9;At least that is the theory. No one who was old enough to remember the battle survived to tell the tale. Khondoran used all his healing powers to save the wounded infant, and still only just succeeded.</P><br><P>&#9;The massive war party assembled by the Wolves of the North was not so fortunate. Their corpses were found in mass…dismembered and scattered about the burnt wreckage of Kheldan’s parents’ house.</P><br><P>&#9;Khondoran had a name for the beast. It was the One Without Form….it was The Doppleganger.</P><br><P>&#9;We talked long about it. For the first time, I recalled the night of Layla’s death clearly.</P><br><P>&#9;As we drank and ate and talked--long into the next night—smoking our tobbacco and reflecting.</P><br><P>&#9;What could this creature do, if it had mind to do so? What sorrow could it reap in Quenos? Or in Halas? Or in all of Norrath?</P><br><P>&#9;This night will live in my mind forever…it is the night my life had been building to for thirty-one years.</P><br><P>&#9;The Erudites say that a life without reflection, is a life unlived. I have found that there is some truth to this—so I shall reflect now--on this night in Halas. </P><br><P>I beleive that going out into Everfrost, to try and earn my keep had empowered me. I was not relegated to Surefall, or Qeynos. I was a hunter—perhaps my technique with polar bears needed some refinement, but I was skilled. The loss of my Layla had made me stronger than I could have been, even as her love brought me alive while she was with me. Gnasher Furgut had taught me, also…the precious value of life and its fleeting nature. And now my new friend Kheldan…a brother by blood, somehow…a brother in the Monster…a brother in the Doppleganger—his presence opened up a world of possibilities. It opened up a future.</P><br><P>As we talked together, the way these things fit together began to become clear to me. I thought back all the way to my thirteeth year, as a boy in Surefall, holding in my hands the enchanted bow of Tamryn Scholl. My fascintation with the bow was not the fascination with the bow itself—it was what that bow represented to me. It was adventure. It was action. It was danger in high places for a higher ideal. It was heroism.</P>         <br>
